[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0m[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong and what I changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
There were [1mtwo bugs[22m in the Rust translation:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 1: Missing exported symbol[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The test runner dynamically loads [38;5;10mlibblake.so[0m and looks up the symbol [38;5;10mSPX_gen_message_random[0m (matching the C macro [38;5;10m#define gen_message_random SPX_NAMESPACE(gen_message_random)[0m). The Rust library never exported this symbol.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mFix in [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m[22m: Added an [38;5;10m#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe extern "C" fn SPX_gen_message_random(...)[0m wrapper that calls the internal [38;5;10mhash_blake::gen_message_random[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 2: Incorrect [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m datalen units (bytes vs bits)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This was the subtle semantic bug. The C [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m function takes [38;5;10mdatalen[0m in [1mbits[22m (it internally does [38;5;10m>> 3[0m to convert to bytes). The C [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m passes raw byte counts directly:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mc
[0m[38;5;10m// C code — passes SPX_N=32 and mlen as-is (interpreted as bits by blake512_update)
blakeX_update(&S, sk_prf, SPX_N);    // 32 bits = 4 bytes hashed
blakeX_update(&S, optrand, SPX_N);   // 32 bits = 4 bytes hashed
blakeX_update(&S, m, mlen);          // mlen bits hashed
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translation incorrectly "fixed" this by multiplying by 8, thinking it was converting bytes to bits:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10m// WRONG — hashes 256 bits = 32 bytes instead of 32 bits = 4 bytes
blake512_update(&mut s, &sk_prf[..SPX_N], (SPX_N as u64) * 8);
